    "    \"## 9. Model Comparison — Key Conclusions\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Decision | Rationale |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"|----------|-----------|\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **Primary metric: PR-AUC** | ROC-AUC is inflated by 98% true-negative class; PR-AUC focuses on fraud class performance |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **F2 (β=2) for threshold** | Recall weighted 2× — missing fraud costs $15k; false alert costs $5 |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **SMOTE sampling strategy 0.1** | 1:10 minority:majority is realistic; 50/50 distorts prior and inflates recall |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **TimeSeriesSplit** | Prevents temporal leakage — train on past, test on future |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **Ensemble > single model** | Statistical catches rules violations; unsupervised catches novel patterns; supervised maximises recall; LLM provides contextual reasoning |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| **Optimal threshold ≠ 0.5** | Always tune threshold via PR curve sweep |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Interview-ready model selection rationale\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"> *\\\"I use XGBoost as the best supervised model: scale-invariant (no need to scale features), handles class imbalance via scale_pos_weight, and supports SHAP explanations for compliance. For production, I'd ensemble it with IsolationForest (unsupervised — catches novel fraud patterns without labels) and the LLM agent (contextual reasoning across user history and fraud typologies). The decision engine fuses all three with configurable YAML weights so compliance teams can adjust without code changes.\\\"*\"\n",
